<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hessian Ludwig Railway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1845">1845</a>–<a href="/years/1897">1897</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Hessische Ludwigsbahn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hessian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-railway">Ludwig Railway</a> was a private railway company of the Grand Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, named after Grand Duke Louis III. Founded in 1845 and based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, it built lines linking Mainz with Worms, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>. It was taken over in 1897 by the joint Prussian and Hessian state railways.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hessian-ludwig-railway.jpg" alt="Hessian Ludwig Railway" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ludwigsbahnhof_Darmstadt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The company grew out of a gap in the railway policy of the Grand Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>. Starkenburg had the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/main-neckar-railway">Main-Neckar Railway</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-hesse">Upper Hesse</a> the Main-Weser Railway, but the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhenish-hesse">Rhenish Hesse</a> had no line. The first push came from outside: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarian-palatinate">Bavarian Palatinate</a>, which licensed railway building in 1844, wanted a link northwards, and the railway pioneer Friedrich List championed a line from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> to Worms, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, for strategic reasons, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> opposed it. The government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a>, which had passed a law for state railways in 1842, at first held back.</p><p>Because the state stayed out, the line was left to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joint-stock-company">joint-stock company</a>, based not in the capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a>, but in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhenish-hesse">Rhenish Hesse</a>. From 1845 some in the government favoured a private railway for the province, and on 15 August 1845 a licence was granted to the Mainz-Ludwigshafen Railway Company, later renamed in honour of the grand duke, though he had at first opposed the line.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Shareholders withdrew money, and work begun in spring 1848 nearly stopped when the revolution of 1848 emptied the state treasury; only in August 1852 did the Hessian government agree to support the company, and a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> covered the whole line. The 46-kilometre route opened in sections from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> to Worms between 23 March and 24 August 1853, and through trains ran to Ludwigshafen from 15 November 1853. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt-rhine-main">Rhine-Main</a> line from Mainz through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aschaffenburg">Aschaffenburg</a> opened on 1 August 1858, crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> by train ferry until a bridge built by MAN-Werk Gustavsburg opened on 1 December 1862.</p><p>Further lines followed, among them the West Rhine Railway to Bingen in 1859, the Main Railway from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> in 1863, the Riedbahn from 1869, the Main-Lahn Railway to Limburg in 1877 and the Odenwald Railway in 1882, until the company had a network of 697 kilometres. It began with six locomotives from the Maschinenfabrik Esslingen, named after figures such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-gutenberg">Johannes Gutenberg</a>, and by the end of 1895 it had 216 locomotives and 544 coaches.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The company became the main provider of rail services in the two southern provinces of the grand duchy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhenish-hesse">Rhenish Hesse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/starkenburg">Starkenburg</a>, and one of the largest privately owned railways in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. On 1 April 1897 it was nationalised and joined the Prussian-Hessian Railway Operating and Financial Association. Local people said that its initials, HLB, stood for Hoch lebe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Bismarck</a>, long live Bismarck.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hessian Ludwig Railway” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Main-Neckar Railway linking Frankfurt, Darmstadt and Heidelberg opened in 1846, and the private <b>Hessian Ludwig Railway</b> connected Mainz with the Rhineland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-hesse">Grand Duchy of Hesse · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
